# Valgrind suppressions for ghoti.io-text.
#
# VALGRIND_FLAGS in the Makefile has passed --suppressions=tools/valgrind.supp
# since the file was introduced, and this file has never existed in the
# repository: `tools` was listed in .gitignore, so nothing under it was ever
# committed. Valgrind treats a missing suppressions file as fatal - it prints
# "FATAL: can't open suppressions file" and exits 1 before running anything -
# so `make test-valgrind` and `make test-valgrind-quiet` could not have
# succeeded for anyone, on any machine, in that time.
#
# The set was deliberately empty when this file was introduced. It has one
# entry now, below. Add another only for a leak that is demonstrably not ours -
# one in libc, the dynamic loader, or Google Test - or for a diagnostic that a
# test provokes on purpose and cannot provoke any other way, and say in a
# comment which, and why it cannot be fixed on our side.

# Memcheck calls a realloc size with the top bit set "fishy", on the reading
# that it is probably a negative number that was converted. Four tests in
# tests/test-json.cpp - the JsonBufferGrow overflow cases - ask
# json_buffer_grow_unified() for exactly such a size on purpose: the growth
# arithmetic overflows, the function falls back to the caller's `needed`, and
# the point of the test is that realloc then *fails* and the buffer and its
# capacity are left alone. The size has to be near SIZE_MAX for the multiply
# to overflow at all, so there is no version of these tests that exercises the
# path without tripping this check.
#
# This is not a leak and not somebody else's code, which the policy above did
# not anticipate. It is an intentional argument to an allocator that is
# expected to refuse it. Without this entry `make test-valgrind` has been red
# since the growth tests landed, and a gate that is always red is a gate
# nobody reads.
{
   json_buffer_grow_unified asks realloc for a size it expects to be refused
   Memcheck:FishyValue
   realloc(size)
   fun:realloc
   fun:json_buffer_grow_unified
}
